# The three experiments opened by gate G-0 of `plans/plan-z32-transform.html`

`plans/note-z32transform-X.html` is the write-up; `Z32/xg0.py` regenerates every number in it
into `Z32/data/xg0_*.txt`.  This file is the part the kernel checks: three certificates, all
`decide`-checked, no `native_decide`, no cited axiom.

## X-238 — the two-cell family, and the `1/5` ceiling that was not one

`Z32/BlockCert.lean` carries `Z32.two_cell_fifth_empty`: no `ξ ≠ 0` has `‖ξ(3/2)ⁿ‖ < 1/5` for
every `n`.  Gate G-0 found that this is behind print — [Dub06JNT], quoted as Theorem 3.14 of
Bugeaud's Cambridge Tract 193, gives every `ξ ≠ 0` a limit point of `(‖ξ(3/2)ⁿ‖)` at least
`0.238117…` — and the engine was reported as failing already at `c = 0.21`.

That report was an artifact of the *default* certificate search, which merges the surviving
components into interval hulls.  The **rank-stratified** search — the same `funcOk` relaxation
that `Z32.dubickas_2008_cor_1_2` needs — certifies `c = 0.21` at funnel depth **1**, and climbs
to `c = 0.238` at depth 7.  `Z32.two_cell_238_empty` is that entry, and it supersedes
`Z32.two_cell_fifth_empty`.

The mechanism is exact and is a statement about the interval model, not about the search: the
two-cell hold set acquires its periodic orbits one at a time, at

| orbit | period | enters `‖·‖ < c` at |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{0}` | 1 | `0` |
| `{1/5, 4/5}` | 2 | `1/5 = 0.2` |
| `{2/13, 3/13, 10/13, 11/13}` | 4 | `3/13 = 0.230769…` |
| `{14,17,21,23,74,76,80,83}/97` | 8 | `23/97 = 0.237113…` |
| one orbit, denominator `3¹⁶−2¹⁶` | 16 | `10233015/42981185 = 0.238081…` |
| two orbits, denominator `3¹²−2¹²` | 12 | `25115/105469 = 0.238126…` |

— a period-doubling cascade — and the funnel a certificate needs grows with the orbit count
(depth 1 with two orbits, 7 with four, 15 with five).  Measured, the ceiling of the search lies
in `(0.2381175, 0.2381177]`, and the printed constant `0.238117…` lies in `[0.238117, 0.238118)`:
the two brackets **overlap**, so print is not beaten, but the gap G-0 priced at `0.038` is
`2·10⁻⁶`.  Whether the two numbers coincide is an open question about [Dub06JNT]'s proof.

## X-D19 — [Dub19] Theorem 1.2, missed by one grid unit

Dubickas proves `Z_{3/2}(8/57, 805/1539) = ∅`, a window of length `31/81 = 0.38271…`.  The engine
refuses it at every depth to 61, in both modes, and the reason is a single point: the **left
endpoint** `8/57` satisfies `(3·8/57)/2 = 4/19`, so it is a preimage of the 3-cycle
`4/19 → 6/19 → 9/19` — the very cycle that the corpus's own `Z32.ZSet_three_two_sixth_3_8` is
built around.  Its backward orbit inside the window is infinite, the component count is exactly
`k+1` at every depth `k`, and no finite rank stratification exists.

Move that endpoint by **one unit of `1/1539`** and the certificate appears at depth 11 with three
blocks: `Z32.ZSet_three_two_d19_shift`, length `196/513 = 0.38206…`.  Shrinking the *right*
endpoint instead changes nothing at all, at any value tested.  So the printed theorem is sharp at
its left end by construction, and that sharpness — not the length — is what this certificate class
cannot pay for.

## X-KP — Mahler's conjecture is out of reach of this family, provably

[KP18] Corollary 18 reduces Mahler's problem to the emptiness of `U = [0,1/6) ∪ [1/3,2/3)`, of
total length only `1/2`.  The hold set of that union is

`H = [0, 1/15) ∪ [1/3, 2/5) ∪ [5/9, 3/5)`, of measure `8/45`,

which is **exactly invariant** (a finite check: `prune(H, H) = H`), so no depth ever empties it,
and whose transition graph has characteristic polynomial `x(x² - x - 1)`: the golden-mean shift.
Its periodic points of period dividing `n` are the Lucas numbers `1, 3, 4, 7, 11, 18, 29, …`, so
the hold set carries **infinitely many periodic orbits** — and Theorem B of `Z32/README.md`
milestone M7 then forbids every block certificate for it, archimedean or product, at every level.

`Z32.horseKP` is the quantitative form: twenty-one words of length eight on `K = [0, 399/6305]`,
so `φ_model ≥ (log 21)/8 = 0.38063…`, against the true `log((1+√5)/2) = 0.48121…`.  Through
`Z32.not_cert_and_horse` it also gives the no-go directly for unstratified certificates.
-/

